"Journal of Combinatorics (https://intlpress.com/api/bgcloud-front/resource/pdf/volume/1805864008446062594-1805864008446062594-e98519905e764bf9b1855922c5d672b0.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Schroeder ... This conjecture remains open, though there is strong evidence to suggest ... has a prime labeling if and only if G has at most one odd component.\nJournal of Combinatorics\nVolume 12, Number 3, 379–388, 2021\nA 2-regular graph has a prime labeling if and only if\nit has at most one odd component\nJ. Z. Schroeder\nA graph G is prime if the vertices can be distinctly labeled with the\nintegers 1, 2, . . . , |V (G)| so that adjacent vertices have relatively\nprime labels. In this paper, we prove the long-standing conjecture\nthat a 2-regular graph G is prime if and only if G has at most one\nodd component.\nAMS 2000 subject classifications: 05C78.\nKeywords and phrases: Prime labeling, regular graph, graph labeling.\n1. Introduction\nNS4mUqRhOX+lEhZdvEDIsJmKsJ1s4hSdnUspy3a7cQo=\nFor a simple graph G with vertex set V (G) = {v1, v2, . . . , vn} and edge set\nE(G), a prime labeling of G is a bijection f : V (G) →{1, 2, . . . , n} such\nthat f(vi) and f(vj) are relatively prime for all {vi, vj} ∈E(G) (see Fig-\nure 1). A graph is called prime if it admits a prime labeling. This concept\nwas formally introduced in 1982 by Tout, Dabboucy and Howalla [13]. That\nseminal paper contained two conjectures that have driven much of the study\nof prime labelings; the ﬁrst concerns trees and was originally made by En-\ntriger in 1980.\nConjecture 1.1 (Entriger, see [13]). Every tree has a prime labeling.\nThis conjecture remains open, though there is strong evidence to suggest\nit is true. Pikhurto showed that all trees with at most 50 vertices are prime\n[8, 9] and, together with Haxell and Taraz, that all suﬃciently large trees\nare prime [7]. Moreover, a number of special families of trees are known to\nbe prime, including complete binary trees [5]; spiders, palm trees, banana\ntrees, and binomial trees [10]; and, trivially, paths and stars.\nThe second conjecture from [13] proposes a classiﬁcation for the primal-\nity of 2-regular graphs.\nConjecture 1.2 (Tout, Dabboucy and Howalla [13]). A 2-regular graph G\nhas a prime labeling if and only if G has at most one odd component.\n379\nNS4mUqRhOX+lEhZdvEDIsJmKsJ1s4hSdnUspy3a7cQo=--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS (https://archive.intlpress.com/site/pub/files/_fulltext/journals/joc/2024/0015/0001/JOC-2024-0015-0001-e007.pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Schroeder*After publication of the research article named above, the author ... current status of the conjecture that a 2-regular graph has a prime ... KEYWORDS AND PHRASES: Prime labeling, regular graph, graph labeling.1. ... labels are placed in order based on smallest odd prime factor (i.e. first all\nJOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS\nVolume 15, Number 1, 135–137, 2024Correction to “A 2-regular graph has a prime\nlabeling if and only if it has at most one odd\ncomponent”\n[Journal of Combinatorics 12 (2021) 379–388]J. Z. Schroeder*After publication of the research article named above, the author\ndiscovered an error in the algorithm used to prove both Theorem\n2.1 (cited from another paper by the current author) and Theorem\n3.5. This error has a major impact on the stated results of the\noriginal paper. This erratum explains the error and details the\ncurrent status of the conjecture that a 2-regular graph has a prime\nlabeling if and only if it has at most one odd component.AMS 2000 SUBJECT CLASSIFICATIONS: 05C78.\nKEYWORDS AND PHRASES: Prime labeling, regular graph, graph labeling.1.  Explanation of the errorIn the labeling algorithm used to prove Theorem 3.5 in [2], the required\nlabels are placed in order based on smallest odd prime factor (i.e. first all\nthe multiples of 3 are placed on open vertices, then the remaining multiples\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrime labeling of 2-regular graphs (https://msgt.fis.unm.si/Prog2017.pdf)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 7 months ago; Schroeder ... It was conjectured in 1982 that a 2-regular graph, which must be a union of cycles, has a prime labeling if and only if at most one of the cycles has odd length. We will discuss how the author’s recent result that every cubic bipartite graph has a prime labeling applies to 2-regular graphs, and we will close with a strategy for providing a complete resolution to the original conjecture.\nPrime labeling of 2-regular graphs\n\nJustin Z. Schroeder\n\n**Abstract**\n\nA prime labeling of a graph \\(G\\) on \\(n\\) vertices is a bijection \\(f : V(G) \\rightarrow \\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}\\) such that \\(f(v)\\) and \\(f(w)\\) are relatively prime for every edge \\(vw\\). It was conjectured in 1982 that a 2-regular graph, which must be a union of cycles, has a prime labeling if and only if at most one of the cycles has odd length. We will discuss how the author’s recent result that every cubic bipartite graph has a prime labeling applies to 2-regular graphs, and we will close with a strategy for providing a complete resolution to the original conjecture.\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe transition conjecture for unicyclic graphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/346257/the-transition-conjecture-for-unicyclic-graphs)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; This conjecture asks whether the space of unicyclic graphs with a fixed degree sequence is connected under 2-switches; the source presents this as an interesting case where a transition theorem may exist, without giving a resolution. ... Jaume, Adrián Pastine and Victor Nicolas Schvöllner, “2-switch: transition and stability on graphs and forests”, arXiv:2004.11164 (2020).\n\nThe transition conjecture for unicyclic graphs\n\nAbout 6 years old · traced to 2020\n\nLet $U$U and $U'$U′ be unicyclic graphs with the same degree sequence. A 2-switch is an edge-switching operation on a graph. Transition conjecture. There is a sequence of 2-switches transforming $U$U into $U'$U′ such that every intermediate graph is also unicyclic. This conjecture asks whether the space of unicyclic graphs with a fixed degree sequence is connected under 2-switches; the source presents this as an interesting case where a transition theorem may exist, without giving a resolution.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nDaniel A. Jaume, Adrián Pastine and Victor Nicolas Schvöllner, “2-switch: transition and stability on graphs and forests”, arXiv:2004.11164 (2020).\n\ndegree-sequencesgraph-theory\n\n0 0\n\n3 weeks ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2-switch transition on unicyclic graphs and pseudoforest (https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.00618)\nciteturn1academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.7 years ago; Title: 2-switch transition on unicyclic graphs and pseudoforest\nTitle: 2-switch transition on unicyclic graphs and pseudoforest\nAuthors: Daniel A. Jaume, Adrián Pastine, Victor Schvöllner\nDate: Sun Feb 28 21:05:21 2021\n\nIn the present work we prove that given any two unicycle graphs (pseudoforests) that share the same degree sequence there is a finite sequence of 2-switches transforming one into the other such that all the graphs in the sequence are also unicyclic graphs (pseudoforests).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA $2$-regular graph has a prime labeling if and only if it has at most one odd component | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/363353986_A_2-regular_graph_has_a_prime_labeling_if_and_only_if_it_has_at_most_one_odd_component)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Justin Zane Schroeder ... In the labeling algorithm used to prove Theorem 3.5 in [2], the required labels are placed in order based on smallest odd prime factor (i.e. first all the multiples of 3 are placed on open vertices, then the remaining multiples of 5, and so on). ...\n\n# A 2-regular graph has a prime labeling if and only if it has at most one odd component\n\n  * January 2021\n  * Journal of Combinatorics 12(3):379-388\n\nDOI:10.4310/JOC.2021.v12.n3.a1\n\nAuthors:\n\nJustin Zane Schroeder\n\n  * Dakota State University\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the author.\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the author.\n\n\n## Citations (1)\n\n## References (0)\n\n... In the labeling algorithm used to prove Theorem 3.5 in [2], the required labels are placed in order based on smallest odd prime factor (i.e. first all the multiples of 3 are placed on open vertices, then the remaining multiples of 5, and so on). ...\n\n... For a given prime p and label 1 such that p| 1 but p | 1 for all odd primes p < p, 1 must be placed on an unlabeled vertex v such that for any neighbor of v labeled with 2 , the greatest common divisor of 1 and 2 is 1. The algorithm given in [2] ensures that 1 and 2 have no common prime factors less than or equal to p, but it fails to exclude the possibility of a common prime factor greater than p. For example, when placing the even multiples of 5 in the fourth paragraph of the proof of Theorem 3.5, the algorithm ensures that the vertices receiving these labels have no neighbors that were previously labeled with a multiple of 15, but it fails to account for the possibility that a new label 1 might share a factor larger than 5 with a previously placed label 2 , such as 1 = 110 and 2 = 33. ...\n\n... Corollary 2.3 and Theorem 3.5 in [2] have been nullified, and the impact of this is addressed in the following section. ...\n\nCorrection to “A 2-regular graph has a prime labeling if and only if it has at most one odd component” [ Journal of Combinatorics 12 (2021) 379–388]\n\n  * Jan 2024\n\n  * Justin Zane Schroeder\n\nView--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrime labelings of generalized Petersen graphs and large cubic bipartite graphs (https://www.math.fau.edu/combinatorics/SchluchterSteven.pdf)\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.8 years ago; Schroeder, T. ... In this talk we will develop a prime labeling algorithm that has verified the conjecture for all even \\(n\\) and odd \\(k\\) such that \\(2 \\le n \\le 9000\\) and all possible odd \\(k\\).\nPrime labelings of generalized Petersen graphs and large cubic bipartite graphs\n\nSteven Schluchter* and Thomas Wilson, George Mason University\n\nA graph \\(G\\) is called *prime* if there exists a labeling of the vertices of \\(G\\) with distinct labels \\(1,2,\\ldots,|V(G)|\\) such that the labels on any two adjacent vertices are relatively prime; such a labeling is called a *prime labeling*. In general, bipartiteness is neither necessary nor sufficient for a graph to be prime. S. Schluchter, J.Z. Schroeder, T. Wilson, et. al. conjectured that a generalized Petersen graph \\(P(n,k)\\) is prime if and only if it is bipartite, which occurs for \\(n\\) even and \\(k\\) odd, and they verified that the conjecture holds for all even \\(n \\le 50\\) and all possible odd \\(k\\). We give more evidence to support this conjecture. In this talk we will develop a prime labeling algorithm that has verified the conjecture for all even \\(n\\) and odd \\(k\\) such that \\(2 \\le n \\le 9000\\) and all possible odd \\(k\\). We also develop a different prime labeling method that we have used to prime label all cubic bipartite graphs \\(G\\) such that \\(8 \\le |V(G)| \\le 22\\).\n\nKeywords: graph labeling, generalized Petersen graph, prime graph.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMichael Schroeder (https://michaelschroeder.ai/)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; ### Noncoverage for Distinct Odd Moduli with at Most Three Prime Divisors\nThe proofs combine geometric arguments with exact computation and are formalized in Lean. The conjectured thresholds of 33 and 65 remain open.\n\nRead the paper PDF Supporting materials Zenodo DOI\n\nSep 9, 2026 · Revised Sep 15 · Number Theory · Combinatorics\n\n### Noncoverage for Distinct Odd Moduli with at Most Three Prime Divisors\n\nWe prove that distinct odd moduli cannot cover the integers when each modulus has at most three distinct prime divisors. Prime powers, residues, and the total number of primes across the family are unrestricted. The main theorem is formalized in Lean. A separate extension requires a modulus with at least four distinct prime divisors, all at most one billion.\n\nRead the paper PDF Supporting materials Zenodo DOI\n\nSep 13, 2026 · Number Theory · Combinatorics\n\n### Nine Prime Divisors in Odd Distinct Covering Systems\n\nWe show that any odd distinct covering system must involve at least nine distinct primes, strengthening our earlier bound of eight. The proof allows arbitrary prime powers and is formalized in Lean, together with two quantitative corollaries. The paper also examines the method’s limits at rank ten.\n\nRead the paper PDF Supporting materials Zenodo DOI\n\nSep 6, 2026 · Number Theory · Combinatorics\n\n### Eight Prime Divisors in Odd Distinct Covering Systems\n\nWe advance research on the Erdős–Selfridge odd covering problem and show that any odd distinct covering system must involve at least eight distinct primes, improving the previous bound of seven. The proof allows arbitrary prime powers and is formalized in Lean. A separate formalization shows that the paper’s specified scalar method cannot reach a nine-prime bound.\n\nRead the paper PDF Supporting materials Zenodo DOI\n\nSep 1, 2026 · Number Theory · Combinatorics\n\n### Seven Prime Divisors in Odd Distinct Covering Systems\n\nThe Erdős–Selfridge problem, dating to 1965, asks whether finitely many arithmetic progressions with distinct odd moduli greater than one can cover the integers. This paper advances the frontier on this longstanding problem by proving that any such system must have at least seven distinct prime divisors. A Lean formalization and an independent exact-rational checker accompany the proof.\n\nRead the paper PDF Supporting materials Zenodo DOI\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Prime labelings of generalized Petersen graphs (https://www.academia.edu/63730731/Prime_labelings_of_generalized_Petersen_graphs)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; For k > 1, an algorithmic approach to labeling P (n, k) is demonstrated; using this approach, we can show P (n, k) is prime for all even n ≤ 50 and all odd 3 ≤ k < n/2. ... In this paper, these questions are answered partially and also show certain families of graphs that admit prime distance labeling (PDL) if and only if the Twin Prime Conjecture holds, besides establishing PDL of some special graphs. ... Justin Schroeder and Steven Schluchter) , George Mason University, Fairfax VA – 22030 Abstract A prime labeling of a graph G on n vertices is an assignment of the integers 1, 2, ..., n to the vertices of G such that the labels on adjacent vertices are relatively prime; if G admits a prime labeling then we say G is a prime graph.\n\n# Prime labelings of generalized Petersen graphs\n\nJustin Schroeder\n\n2017, Involve, a Journal of Mathematics\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.2140/INVOLVE.2017.10.109\n\nvisibility\n\n…\n\ndescription\n\n1 page\n\ndownload Download PDF\n\n## Abstract\n\nA prime labeling of a graph G on n vertices is an assignment of the integers 1, 2, ..., n to the vertices of G such that the labels on adjacent vertices are relatively prime; if G admits a prime labeling then we say G is a prime graph. In this talk, we explore the problem of determining if the generalized Petersen graph P (n, k) is prime. A prime labeling of P (n, 1) for even n is provided on the condition that a certain number theoretic conjecture is true; we have demonstrated via computer that this conjecture-which bears resemblance to Bertrand's Postulate and the Goldbach Conjecture-is true for all even n ≤ 2×10 9. For k > 1, an algorithmic approach to labeling P (n, k) is demonstrated; using this approach, we can show P (n, k) is prime for all even n ≤ 50 and all odd 3 ≤ k < n/2.\nA graph G is a prime distance graph (PDL) iff there exists a \"prime distance labeling\" (PDL) of G. In this paper, we obtain the prime labeling and prime distance labeling of certain classes of graphs.\n\nPrime labelings of generalized Petersen graphs Kathryn Cokus, Ryan Ellingson, and Thomas Wilson (co. Justin Schroeder and Steven Schluchter) , George Mason University, Fairfax VA – 22030 Abstract A prime labeling of a graph G on n vertices is an assignment of the integers 1, 2, ..., n to the vertices of G such that the labels on adjacent vertices are relatively prime; if G admits a prime labeling then we say G is a prime graph. In this talk, we explore the problem of determining if the generalized Petersen graph P (n, k) is prime. A prime labeling of P (n, 1) for even n is provided on the condition that a certain number theoretic conjecture is true; we have demonstrated via computer that this conjecture — which bears resemblance to Bertrand’s Postulate and the Goldbach Conjecture — is true for all even n ≤ 2×109 . For k > 1, an algorithmic approach to labeling P (n, k) is demonstrated; using this approach, we can show P (n, k) is prime for all even n ≤ 50 and all odd 3 ≤ k < n/2. Keywords: simple graph, prime labeling, generalized Petersen graph.\n\nAbout the author\n\nImage\n\nJustin Schroeder\n\nadd Follow mail Message\n\nPapers\n\n18\n\nFollowers\n\n5\n\nView all papers from Justin Schroederarrow_forward\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThree-prime-divisor paper and proof materials · Michael Schroeder (https://michaelschroeder.ai/research/ThreePrimeDivisors/companion)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; Michael Schroeder · Preprint · Version 1.0 · 9 September 2026 · Revised 15 September 2026 · ORCID 0009-0004-3249-0195 ... Thus every hypothetical odd distinct covering has a modulus with at least four distinct prime divisors, all at most 10^{9}.\n\nMichael Schroeder · Preprint · Version 1.0 · 9 September 2026 · Revised 15 September 2026 · ORCID 0009-0004-3249-0195\n\n# Noncoverage for Distinct Odd Moduli with at Most Three Prime Divisors\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe prove that a finite family of congruence classes with pairwise distinct odd moduli greater than one cannot cover the integers if each modulus has at most three distinct prime divisors. The prime powers, residue classes, and total number of primes occurring across the family are unrestricted. A normalized distortion measure and a conditional convex comparison reduce the problem to a count of one-prime and two-prime cofactors. Twenty-eight factorial moments close under a positive recurrence; exact rational arithmetic through prime 997 and a bound for all larger primes give total charge less than 0.937076 < 15/16. We also prove that the three-divisor restriction is needed only for moduli whose largest prime divisor is at most 10^{9}. Thus every hypothetical odd distinct covering has a modulus with at least four distinct prime divisors, all at most 10^{9}. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Macajova_E in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AMacajova_E+in%3Amath)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Apr 30 2020 math.CO arXiv:2004.14049v1 ... @misc{2004.14049, author = {Edita Máčajová and Giuseppe Mazzuoccolo and Vahan Mkrtchyan and Jean Paul Zerafa}, title = {{S}ome snarks are worse than others}, year = {2020}, eprint = {2004.14049}, note = {arXiv:2004.14049v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n  * Some snarks are worse than others\n\nEdita Máčajová, Giuseppe Mazzuoccolo, Vahan Mkrtchyan, Jean Paul Zerafa\n\nApr 30 2020 math.CO arXiv:2004.14049v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n1\n\n@misc{2004.14049, author = {Edita Máčajová and Giuseppe Mazzuoccolo and Vahan Mkrtchyan and Jean Paul Zerafa}, title = {{S}ome snarks are worse than others}, year = {2020}, eprint = {2004.14049}, note = {arXiv:2004.14049v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nMany conjectures and open problems in graph theory can either be reduced to cubic graphs or are directly stated for cubic graphs. Furthermore, it is known that for a lot of problems, a counterexample must be a snark, i.e. a bridgeless cubic graph which is not 3--edge-colourable. In this paper we deal with the fact that the family of potential counterexamples to many interesting conjectures can be narrowed even further to the family ${\\cal S}_{\\geq 5}$ of bridgeless cubic graphs whose edge set cannot be covered with four perfect matchings. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Schvollner_V in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ASchvollner_V+in%3Amath)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago;   * 2-switch transition on unicyclic graphs and pseudoforest\n  * 2-switch transition on unicyclic graphs and pseudoforest\n\nDaniel A. Jaume, Adrián Pastine, Victor Schvöllner\n\nMar 02 2021 math.CO arXiv:2103.00618v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2103.00618, author = {Daniel A.~Jaume and Adrián Pastine and Victor Schvöllner}, title = {2-switch transition on unicyclic graphs and pseudoforest}, year = {2021}, eprint = {2103.00618}, note = {arXiv:2103.00618v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIn the present work we prove that given any two unicycle graphs (pseudoforests) that share the same degree sequence there is a finite sequence of 2-switches transforming one into the other such that all the graphs in the sequence are also unicyclic graphs (pseudoforests).\n\n  * Spectral ordering and 2-switch transformations\n\nElismar R. Oliveira, Victor N. Schvöllner, Vilmar Trevisan\n\nJun 25 2020 math.CO arXiv:2006.13870v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2006.13870, author = {Elismar R.~Oliveira and Victor N.~Schvöllner and Vilmar Trevisan}, title = {{S}pectral ordering and 2-switch transformations}, year = {2020}, eprint = {2006.13870}, note = {arXiv:2006.13870v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe address the problem of ordering trees with the same degree sequence by their spectral radii. To achieve that, we consider 2-switch transformations which preserve the degree sequence and establish when the index decreases. Our main contribution is to determine a total ordering of a particular family by their indices according to a given parameter related to sizes in the tree.\n\n  * 2-switch: transition and stability on graphs and forests\n\nDaniel A. Jaume, Adrián Pastine, Victor Nicolas Schvöllner\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) 2-switch: transition and satability on forests and pseudofests (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/401721156_2-switch_transition_and_satability_on_forests_and_pseudofests)\nciteturn1search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; 2-switch: transition and stability on forests and ... A 2-switch τ on a unicyclic graph U is said to be a u-switch if τ(U) is\n\nunicyclic. Next, we characterize the u-switches.\n\n7\n\nTheorem 4.3. Let τ be a 2-switch between two disjoint edges e 1, e 2 of a\n\nunicyclic graph U. Then, the following statements hold:\n\n1. If e 1, e 2∈E(For(U)), then τ is a u-switch over U if and only if τ is a\n\nt-switch over U−e, for all e∈E(Cyc(U)).\n\n2. If e 1∈E(Cyc(U)) and e 2∈E(For(U)), then τ is a u-switch over U.\n\n3. If e 1, e 2∈E(Cyc(U)), then τ is a u-switch over U if and only if\n\nτ(Cyc(U)) ≈Cyc(U).\n\nProof. (1). (⇐) By Lemma 4.1,e /∈τ(U−e). Since τ(U−e) is a tree,\n\nτ(U−e)+e=τ(U) is a unicyclic graph.\n\n(⇒)If e 1, e 2∈E(For(U)), e∈E(Cyc(U)) and τ is not a t-switch in\n\nU−e, then τ disconnects U.\n\n(2). By Lemma 4.2, there exists e∈E(Cyc(U)) −e 1 such that e /∈τ(U−e).\n\nClearly, U−e is a tree. If τ(U−e) is a tree, then τ(U−e)+e=\n\nτ(U) is a unicyclic graph. Otherwise, τ(U−e) has two components: a\n\nunicyclic graph U′and a tree T. Since e links U′to T, we have that\n\nτ(U−e)+e=τ(U) is a unicyclic graph.\n\n(3). Let C be a cycle. If |C|= 3, we cannot apply any 2-switch on C. If\n\n|C| ∈ {4,5}, then τ(C)≈C(the symbol ≈denotes the isomorphism\n\nrelation), for every 2-switch τ on C. If |C|≥6, then either τ(C)≈C\n\nor τ(C) is the union of two disjoint cycles.\n\n(⇒) If τ(Cyc(U)) ≈ Cyc(U), then τ disconnects U.\n\n(⇐) If τ(Cyc(U)) ≈Cyc(U), then τ(U) is obviously unicyclic.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCompleting partial transversals of Cayley tables of Abelian groups (https://geodesic.mathdoc.fr/articles/10.37236/9386/)\nciteturn1search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Jaromy Kuhl^{1} ; Donald McGinn^{1} ; Michael William Schroeder^{2} ... Cavenagh, Hämäläinen, and Nelson (in 2009) showed the conjecture is true when $k = 3$ and $n$ is prime. In this paper, we prove Grüttmüller’s conjecture for $k = 2$ and $k = 3$ by establishing a more general result for Cayley tables of Abelian groups of odd ord